    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <section class="chapter" id="ch7">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 7 — The Streaming Architecture</h2>

        <div class="concept">
            <h3>SSE vs WebSockets</h3>
            <table class="info-table">
                <tr><th></th><th>SSE (ours)</th><th>WebSockets</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Direction</td><td>Server → client only</td><td>Bidirectional</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Complexity</td><td>Just HTTP with special headers</td><td>Upgrade handshake, frame mgmt</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Reconnect</td><td>Auto-reconnects</td><td>Manual</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Token streaming, live feeds</td><td>Chat apps, multiplayer games</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>We chose SSE because token streaming is <strong>one-directional</strong> — server pushes tokens to client.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="code-block">
            <h3>The TransformStream Pattern</h3>
            <pre><code>async handleStream(request) &#123;
  // Dispatch tools BEFORE streaming — they feed the prompt
  const toolResults = await this.dispatchTools(message, userContext)

  // Create a pipe: write SSE events → readable end goes to client
  const &#123; readable, writable &#125; = new TransformStream()
  const writer = writable.getWriter()
  const sse = (obj) => writer.write(encode("data: " + JSON.stringify(obj) + "\n\n"))

  // Start streaming in background — NOT awaited
  const streamWork = async () => &#123;
    await sse(&#123; type: "tools", toolsUsed: ["kb_search"] &#125;)  // before first token

    const aiStream = await env.AI.run(MODEL, &#123; messages, stream: true &#125;)
    const reader = aiStream.getReader()

    while (true) &#123;
      const &#123; done, value &#125; = await reader.read()
      if (done) break
      // Parse Workers AI SSE → forward each token to client
      const token = JSON.parse(line.slice(6)).response
      await sse(&#123; type: "token", text: token &#125;)
    &#125;

    await sse(&#123; type: "done", latencyMs: Date.now() - start &#125;)
    await writer.close()
  &#125;

  streamWork()  // fire and forget — don't await!

  // Return readable end immediately — client starts receiving
  return new Response(readable, &#123;
    headers: &#123; "Content-Type": "text/event-stream", "X-Accel-Buffering": "no" &#125;
  &#125;)
&#125;</code></pre>
            <span class="file-ref">📄 src/agents/base-agent.ts:208-376</span>
        </div>

        <div class="questions">
            <h3>Interview Questions</h3>
            <details><summary>Q: How does your streaming architecture work?</summary>
            <div><p>SSE over HTTP. Tools dispatch first, then Workers AI with <code>stream: true</code> returns a ReadableStream. I create a TransformStream — write parsed SSE events to the writable end, return the readable end immediately (without awaiting). Three event types: <code>tools</code> (before first token), <code>token</code> (per word), <code>done</code> (metadata). TTFB under 100ms.</p></div></details>
            <details><summary>Q: Why do tools run before the stream starts?</summary>
            <div><p>Eager dispatch. Tools must complete before the LLM generates because their results feed the prompt. Adds ~500ms before first token, but no LLM → tool → LLM round-trips. Workers AI doesn't reliably support function calling during streaming anyway.</p></div></details>
        </div>
    </section>

