    <section class="chapter" id="ch9">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 9 — The MCP Server</h2>

        <div class="concept">
            <h3>What is MCP?</h3>
            <p><strong>Model Context Protocol</strong> — an open standard from Anthropic for exposing tools to LLM clients (Claude Desktop, Cursor, Continue.dev).</p>
            <pre><code>Claude Desktop → stdio (JSON-RPC) → MCP Server (Node.js)
                                        → HTTPS → SE Intel Worker
                                        → Durable Object agent
                                        ← response back through the chain</code></pre>
            <p>One server works with all MCP-compatible clients. No custom plugin per client.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="code-block">
            <h3>Thin Bridge — All Intelligence Stays in the Worker</h3>
            <pre><code>// The MCP server is ~300 lines. It does 3 things:
// 1. Gets a JWT from the SE Intel Worker
// 2. Forwards tool calls to the Worker API
// 3. Returns the response in MCP format

server.registerTool("research_account", &#123;
  description: "Research a prospect company...",
  inputSchema: &#123;
    company: z.string(),
    role: z.enum(["ae", "se", "csm", "tam", "sales_manager"]).default("se")
  &#125;
&#125;, async (&#123; company, role &#125;) => &#123;
  const token = await getToken(role)  // cached per role
  const resp = await fetch(`$&#123;BASE_URL&#125;/api/v1/account`, &#123;
    headers: &#123; Authorization: `Bearer $&#123;token&#125;` &#125;,
    body: JSON.stringify(&#123; message: company &#125;)
  &#125;)
  return &#123; content: [&#123; type: "text", text: (await resp.json()).response &#125;] &#125;
&#125;)</code></pre>
            <span class="file-ref">📄 projects/se-intel-mcp/src/index.ts:131-175</span>
        </div>

        <div class="questions">
            <h3>Interview Questions</h3>
            <details><summary>Q: What is MCP and why did you build an MCP server?</summary>
            <div><p>Model Context Protocol — open standard from Anthropic for exposing tools to LLM clients. Built an MCP server so SE Intel is accessible from Claude Desktop, Cursor, etc. — not just the web UI. The server is a thin bridge: MCP protocol + token management. All intelligence stays in the Worker.</p></div></details>
            <details><summary>Q: Why is the MCP server a "thin bridge"?</summary>
            <div><p>Single source of truth. All RAG, RBAC, streaming, memory logic lives in the Worker. Update the Worker → MCP picks it up automatically with zero redeployment. Same Worker, three interfaces: web UI, REST API, MCP.</p></div></details>
        </div>
    </section>

